===Etymology===
{{borrowing|en|de|Gestalt|t=shape, figure, form}}. The German term can also apply to a geometric or graphical shape, but that is not the case when this word is used in English.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɡəˈʃtælt/|/ɡəˈʃtɑːlt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ɡəˈʃtɔlt/|/ɡəˈstɔlt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|gestalten}}

# A [[collection]] of [[physical]], [[biological]], [[psychological]] or [[symbolic]] [[element]]s that creates a [[whole]], [[unified]] [[concept]] or [[pattern]] which is [[other]] than the [[sum]] of its [[part]]s, due to the relationships between the parts (of a [[character]], [[personality]], [[entity]], or [[being]])
#: {{ux|en|This biography is the first one to consider fully the writer's '''gestalt'''.}}
#* {{quote-magazine|passage=The clusters of behavioral '''gestalten'''... the probability factors... the subtypes of crimes... the constellations of criminal subtypes...|author=Jay Kirk|title=Watching the Detectives|magazine=Harpers Magazine|volume=307|issue=1839|page=61|date=August 2003}}
#* '''2008''', Jonathan Nasaw, ''Fear Itself''
#*: Obviously it was related to the entire '''gestalt''' of Simon's polyphobia and compensatory counterphobia. The boys used to watch horror movies on late-night television {{...}}
# [[shape|Shape]], [[form]]
#* {{quote-book|passage=Mary did not approve of the Eleanor '''gestalt'''. "I been to Woonsocket S.D., Eleanor McGovern's hometown," she said, "and nobody there? I mean nobody? dresses like that."|author=John L. Hess and Karen Hess|title=The Taste of America|publisher=Grossman|location=New York|year=1977}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=... depending on the kinds of speech children hear directed to them, they may first learn unanalyzed "'''gestalts'''" (e.g., social expressions like "What's that?" uttered as a single unit) instead of learning single words that are then freely recombined ...|author=Kathy Hirsh-Pasek|title=The Origins of Grammar|publisher=The MIT Press|location=Cambridge, MA|year=1996}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=So different were our appearances and approaches and general '''gestalts''' that we had something of an epic rivalry from '74 through '77.|author=David Foster Wallace|title=A Supposedly Fun Thing I'll Never Do Again|location=Boston|publisher=Little, Brown and Co.|edition=1st Back Bay|year=1998}}

